  "updated": "2026-07-24T17:54:31.361756+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1204 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nStarting to see a little more shower coverage in the area as of 10 \nPM, with the shortwave energy and mid-level frontogenesis in SD \nfinally starting to push into the area. Additional storm development \nwas taking place across northern KS as a low-level jet/moisture \ntransport ramped up. This activity should gradually spread \nnortheastward into the forecast area as we go through the overnight \nhours and into Friday morning. With precipitable water values \napproaching and exceeding 2\" (1.92\" on the 00Z OAX sounding) and \nwarm cloud depths of 4-4.5 km, the potential for efficient rain-\nproducing storms will be there. The big question will be how much \nelevated instability will be available to get higher and more \nimpactful rainfall amounts. Latest guidance suggests 1000 J/kg of \nMUCAPE could creep just north of the NE/KS border (a slight uptick \ncompared to afternoon solutions), meaning increasing potential for \nsome heavier rain. Various CAMs highlight localized areas of 1-2+ \ninches in vicinity of this elevated instability, with totals to the \nnorth more in the range of a few hundredths to half an inch. If a \nspot manages to get repeated rounds, can't rule out some localized \nflooding (10% chance at a given spot). That said, ongoing storms and \nheavy rain in KS may end up keeping things a bit farther south \nafter all, so overall confidence in rainfall amounts is \nsomewhat low, though still think there's potential for an inch \nor 2 near the NE/KS border.\n\nPrecip should gradually push out of the area Friday morning with \nlargely dry weather favored by noon or 1 PM. Clouds will linger a \nbit longer and will keep us cooler for one more day as temperatures \ntop out in the mid 70s to mid 80s from east to west across the area. \nFor the evening and overnight, guidance is in good agreement that we \nsee fog development in a good chunk of the area with southerly flow \nhelping to keep dewpoints around 70, near forecast lows overnight.\n\nThe heat and humidity are then very much on for the weekend into \nearly next week as upper level ridging to our west amplifies \nand slides eastward. Given the persistent southerly flow and \ncontinued cropland evapotranspiration, dewpoints should hover in\nthe 70s. Temperatures may be a little more questionable with \nsome guidance hinting at weak shortwave energy rounding the \nridge and bringing us some cloud cover, if not showers and \nstorms, though still lots of spread in exact timing and track of\nthose. However, even with some clouds, it's still looking hot, \nwith ensembles giving us 850 mb temperatures in the mid to upper\n20s C (90th+ percentile of climatology). As a result, model \nconsensus gives us widespread highs in the 90s and few 100s on \nSaturday, followed by mid 90s to lower 100s Sunday and Monday. \nAs a side note, GFS/GEFS guidance continues to over-mix and \ngive us highs exceeding 110 in many locations. EPS continues to \nlook much more reasonable. All that said, expect widespread heat\nindices nearing 105 on Saturday, followed by 105-115 across \nmuch of the area on Sunday and possibly Monday. Heat Advisories \nare looking likely (60-90% chance) those three days and if \nconfidence increases that we'll see 110+ heat indices multiple \ndays, an Extreme Heat Warning may become necessary (50% chance \nfor portions of southwest IA).\n\nBeyond Monday, we'll start to see more shortwave troughs round\nthe ridge and bring us on and off shower and storm chances. This\nwill also lead to slightly cooler temperatures, but still\nseasonably warm with lower to mid 90s for most well into the\nwork week.",
